To add support for a test listener in Googletest that records the duration and final status of every test, you will need to create a custom listener by inheriting from the `testing::TestEventListener` class and then override the appropriate event callback methods.

Here's a step-by-step guide on how to create a custom listener that records test durations and statuses:

### Step 1: Create a Custom Listener

Create a new class that inherits from `testing::TestEventListener`. In this class, you'll override the `OnTestEnd` method to capture the duration and the final status of each test.

```cpp
#include <gtest/gtest.h>
#include <iostream>
#include <chrono>

class DurationTestListener : public testing::TestEventListener {
public:
    explicit DurationTestListener(testing::TestEventListener* listener)
        : testing::TestEventListener(listener) {}

    void OnTestStart(const testing::TestInfo& test_info) override {
        // Record the start time
        start_time_ = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
        // Call the base class's OnTestStart method
        TestEventListener::OnTestStart(test_info);
    }

    void OnTestEnd(const testing::TestInfo& test_info) override {
        // Call the base class's OnTestEnd method
        TestEventListener::OnTestEnd(test_info);

        // Calculate the duration
        auto end_time = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
        std::chrono::duration<double> duration = end_time - start_time_;
        std::string status = test_info.result()->Passed() ? "PASSED" : "FAILED";

        // Log the duration and status
        std::cout << "Test " << test_info.test_case_name() << "."
                  << test_info.name() << " " 
                  << status << " (duration: " 
                  << duration.count() << " seconds)" << std::endl;
    }

private:
    std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::time_point start_time_;
};
```

### Step 2: Register the Listener

To use your custom listener, you need to add it to the Googletest framework. This can be done in the `main` function of your test program.

```cpp
int main(int argc, char** argv) {
    ::testing::InitGoogleTest(&argc, argv);

    // Get the default listener
    testing::TestEventListeners& listeners = ::testing::UnitTest::GetInstance()->listeners();
    // Remove the default listener (optional)
    delete listeners.Release(listeners.default_result_printer());

    // Add the new listener
    listeners.Append(new DurationTestListener(listeners.Release(listeners.default_result_printer())));

    return RUN_ALL_TESTS();
}
```

### Step 3: Compile and Run

Make sure to compile your code with the Googletest library linked, and run your tests. Your custom listener should now report the duration and final status for each test at the end of each test run.

### Conclusion

This setup allows you to easily track how long each test takes and whether it passed or failed. You can further customize the listener as per your requirements, such as logging to a file or formatting the output differently.

