To add test listener support to the Google Test framework as specified in your request, we will implement an additional event listener that can be registered to receive notifications about test events. We will follow the existing structure and conventions as closely as possible to maintain compatibility and ease of use.

### New Event Listener Implementation

Let's create a new class called `CustomTestListener` which will extend `EmptyTestEventListener`. This listener will simply record messages when specific test events occur and can be used for further assertions in unit tests.

Below is the code to be added to `googletest/test/googletest-listener-test.cc`. The implementation is straightforward and aims to demonstrate the listener functionalities effectively.

```cpp
class CustomTestListener : public testing::EmptyTestEventListener {
public:
    void OnTestProgramStart(const testing::UnitTest& unit_test) override {
        g_events->push_back("CustomTestListener: OnTestProgramStart");
    }

    void OnTestIterationStart(const testing::UnitTest& unit_test, int iteration) override {
        g_events->push_back("CustomTestListener: OnTestIterationStart " + std::to_string(iteration));
    }

    void OnTestSuiteStart(const testing::TestSuite& test_suite) override {
        g_events->push_back("CustomTestListener: OnTestSuiteStart " + test_suite.name());
    }

    void OnTestStart(const testing::TestInfo& test_info) override {
        g_events->push_back("CustomTestListener: OnTestStart " + test_info.test_case_name());
    }

    void OnTestPartResult(const testing::TestPartResult& test_part_result) override {
        g_events->push_back("CustomTestListener: OnTestPartResult - " + test_part_result.summary());
    }

    void OnTestEnd(const testing::TestInfo& test_info) override {
        g_events->push_back("CustomTestListener: OnTestEnd " + test_info.test_case_name());
    }

    void OnTestSuiteEnd(const testing::TestSuite& test_suite) override {
        g_events->push_back("CustomTestListener: OnTestSuiteEnd " + test_suite.name());
    }

    void OnTestIterationEnd(const testing::UnitTest& unit_test, int iteration) override {
        g_events->push_back("CustomTestListener: OnTestIterationEnd " + std::to_string(iteration));
    }

    void OnTestProgramEnd(const testing::UnitTest& unit_test) override {
        g_events->push_back("CustomTestListener: OnTestProgramEnd");
    }
};

// Example of registering the listener in tests
TEST(CustomListenerTest, SampleTest) {
    testing::UnitTest::GetInstance()->listeners().Append(new CustomTestListener);

    // Your test code here
    EXPECT_TRUE(true); // Example expectation

    // At the end, you may verify events stored in g_events as per your requirements.
}
```

### Steps to Integrate:
1. **Add the Listener Class**: Place the above code snippet in `googletest/test/googletest-listener-test.cc`.
2. **Register the Listener**: Ensure that your test cases (or test suite) add an instance of `CustomTestListener` to the `UnitTest` listeners. This can be done within a setup method or each test case, as shown.
3. **Implement Event Logic**: The methods implemented in `CustomTestListener` will capture and log various events throughout the test lifecycle. It can be modified to perform additional logic as needed (like asserting or logging to a file).
4. **Testing the Listener**: Create tests to ensure that the listener correctly records events, using the global `g_events` vector to validate captured messages.

### Final Note
By extending `EmptyTestEventListener`, we've provided a foundation for any additional logic you may want in response to test events. The `g_events` vector acts as a simple logging mechanism, which may be replaced with more sophisticated logging or API-based output in a production scenario. 

Feel free to adjust the listener's functionality based on your specific requirements!

